all by itself - does duplication for inheritance hierarchy for prototype-2 version

When defining the ER schema in json file -> dependencies have to be correctly ordered
Entities, Subclasses order adhering to inheritance hierarchy level, All parent entities need to be defined before weak entites, All participating entities have to be defined
before relationship node

For weak entity, discriminator is not mandatory, but if parent has only 10 tuples, and json says 20 tuples required for weak entity, to workload generator to generate all tuples
without hanging, having a discriminator key allows to reselect same parent tuple, since parent_key, weak entity discriminator_key combination uniquely identifies the tuples

relationship
First entity - entity1
Second entity - entity2
Only for M:N relationship -> define entity1_to_entity2 count - which means an entity1 tuple gets mapped to this many entity2 tuples

To make a weak entity 1:1 with the parent entity(e.g. Cart and Customer - Customer can have only 1 one cart) - define the weak entity without discriminator - hence key consists only of
the customer_id - hence each tuple should be uniquely identified only by customer_id

For each node attribute_list is defined as follows ->
node.attribute_list
strong entity(including subclasses) -> all inherited parent attributes(for subclass) + own attributes
weak entity - pks from parent + own discriminator if exists + own attributes
relationship - pks from 2 entities + own attributes

One definition for select all - select * query
strong entity - all its attributes
strong subclass entity - all attributes inherited from parent/s and its own attributes
relationship - pks from 2 entities and its own attributes
weak entity - pk from parent + own discriminator(if exists) + own attributes
map_select_queries.py
search_algorithm.py
In map_select_queries ->
select * from weak entity is just pks and weak entity attributes:
select * from relationship is just pk mapping between two entities

Second definition - select * query
strong entity - all its attributes
strong subclass entity - all attributes inherited from parent/s and its own attributes
relationship - pks from 2 entities and its own attributes + all attributes from 2 entities
weak entity - pk from parent + own discriminator(if exists) + own attributes + all attributes from parent
map_select_queries_all_attributes_extended.py
search_algorithm_all_attributes.py

entity_or_relationship.node_tables -> defined to map inserts
strong entity(which doesn't belong to a hierarchy)/weak entity/relationships -> inserts happen to mapped_table and its own mvd tables if mvds are separate
strong entity(sub class) -> inserts happen to its own tables and to all parent tables

(entity_or_relationship.select_all_tables - the # of tables initialized for a node differs based on 1st/2nd definition of select *)
entity_or_relationship.select_all_tables -> defined to map select * statement
all tables required to answer select * query

For first definition of select *, node.attribute_list is sufficient to answer query.

For second definition of select *, node.attribute_list may not be sufficient for certain node types(weak entity, relationships)
(this is required for second definition of select * )
entity_or_relationship.select_all_nodes -> all nodes required to answer select * based on the attribute list defined for node
for strong entity(including subclasses) -> full attribute list is defined by the node.attribute_list itself
for weak entity - node itself + all depending entities
for relationship - node itself + both participating entities

A query is mapped to a attribute cover, which is covered by a physical table cover.

For folded relationship/weak entity node with participating entity with node_cover
mapped table of participating entity doesn't fully represent participating entity - need to get union all for node cover
mapped_table for node is set to mapped table of participating entity
Since for both these nodes - a temp table is first created to fold into node cover of participating entity - the temp table creation is based on mapped table
Node tables for a node tells in how many tables a tuple of node is inserted/duplicated
For folded relationship/weak entity - a tuple is inserted exactly once in one of the distributed tables.
Hence node tables should include only a single table - for that again mapped table of participating entity is used - since everything will be mapped to single temp table which will be
joined with all nodes in node cover to fold relationship/weak entity into node cover of participating entity
table_mapping - keeps track of mapping from which nodes insert should happen to a table

in search_algorithm_all_attributes.py
- if node is an entity(contained_all_descendants/all by itself) - node.mapped_table is set to own physical table
- if node has a len(node_cover)>1, node.mapped_table doesn't fully cover all tuples relevant to node, hence need to get all tuples distributed in the node_cover to get coverage
node_cover
- is defined for all entities in hierarchy including root
- gives the minimum coverage of nodes to cover the node
- except for contained_all_descendants and leaf all_by_itself nodes, all other types(contained in/partially/non-leaf all) may require node_cover
- except for contained_all_descendants and leaf all_by_itself nodes, all other types(contained in/partially/non-leaf all) may have a len(node_cover)>1
mapped_tables_list
- is defined for folded weak entities/1:M relationships with parent/participating entity with len(node_cover)>1
- For each table in mapped_tables_list for a node, the node is added to table entry in table_mapping where it shows the nodes mapped to a physical table